for today's classic interview we're  gonna go back to when I spoke to Nell  Bernstein back in June of 2014 and we  spoke specifically about child  incarceration this has been shoved back  into the forefront because of the Trump  family separations and child internments  and now spoke generally about child  incarceration outside of the specific  families at the border paradigm and I  think that's the sort of context that  I'm thinking of reviewing the interview  within let's go back to my interview  Nell Bernstein on child incarceration  from June of 2014 and just apply what  she said in this interview to what we  see going on right now every single day  at the us-mexico border I'm joined today  by Nell Bernstein who's the author of  burning down the house as well as all  alone in the world and a winner of the  White House champion of change award  today we're going to talk about  childhood incarceration in the United  States and now I think a good place to  start would be we've talked about adult  incarceration rates in the US and how  the US is up there with North Korea at  the top - in terms of incarceration  rates for adults if we look at child  incarceration rates where does the u.s.  rank well we're at an interesting  juncture right now because on the one  hand juvenile incarceration rates are  falling significantly they've dropped by  40 percent over the past 10 years at the  same time we are still the number one in  the u.s. I mean in the world for locking  up our kids by a factor of about seven I  think and what types of crimes are  getting juveniles in juvenile  incarceration facilities  well because juvenile incarceration so  often leads to adult incarceration the  important question for me is what type  of crimes lead to that first  incarceration right and there it's  almost always a low-level non-dangerous  offense sometimes you know graffiti and  loitering public drunkenness a marijuana  charge and then once you're in the  system endless probation violations  so not not the super predator that we  were led to believe and are there some I  don't know if crimes is the right word  or as you talked about them status  violations that only apply to juveniles  whereas for adults those those actions  wouldn't necessarily put them into  prison or jail  that's tricky also because there were  some legal changes meant to eradicate  locking kids up for status offenses  particularly running away but a lot of  kids I've met are locked up for  something that happened along with the  status events you know say you run away  and you sleep on the street and that's a  crime so I I think minor offenses some  of what your status offenses would be  the most accurate and for people who  don't know can you explain what a status  offense is and maybe give us some other  examples beyond running away sure a  status offense refers to your status as  a juvenile so it's something that would  not be illegal if you were an adult it's  only illegal because you're a kid so  running away underage drinking  truancy that's oh that's a big one you  know if I don't go to work it doesn't go  well for me but I don't go to jail my  kids make the same choice they're at  risk of a truancy charge that sort of  thing and you talked just very briefly  there about how the initial juvenile  incarceration often leads to  incarceration as an adult so I want to  take that in a couple of pieces because  I think there may be a couple of angles  to that first let's talk about the  sentences how can a juvenile  incarceration sentence turn into an  adult incarceration sentence well here's  what happens and I'm gonna give you a  little bit of background yes this is a  year that really shocked me eighty to  ninety percent of all American teenagers  in confidential interviews will tell you  that they've committed an offense  serious enough under the law to get them  locked up but most of them are not  locked up right they nobody notices they  get a pass  or maybe they get some kind of community  alternative and those kids grow up and  grow out of the impulsivity that led to  that original offense the portion of the  eighty to ninety percent of Americans  who are delinquent apparently who get  incarcerated they don't stay straight  through to adulthood but it's a very  traumatic experience many experienced  abuse physical abuse one in ten are  raped by guards and even without that  it's an environment in which sort of  fulfilling all the developmental tasks  of adolescents are pretty much  impossible and of course you develop a  criminal identity so that's why it's so  often spirals into adult incarceration  along with the stigma and label of being  a delinquent so let's talk a little bit  about that eighty to ninety percent of  American teenagers admit to some  activity that could be classified as a  crime but most will never actually spend  any time incarcerated with adult  incarceration we see the  disproportionate application of  sentences to those who are poor and to  minorities do we see those same factors  playing a role in what what percentage  or which ones of those American  teenagers end up spending time in prison  you've asked exactly the right question  and that's why I use that number pretty  frequently I think that some of us are  more familiar with statistics like a  black youth is five times more likely to  be locked up for the same charge and  with the same background as a white  youth so there are disproportionality  at every step of the system arrest  detention disposition or sentencing  parole probation at every juncture  researchers have found not just  disparate rates but differential  treatment based on your race and to a  lesser degree class so I think the  reason it's so important for me to share  that the great great majority of  teenagers get involved in delinquency is  that it really busts the myth that one  race is more criminal than another it  turns out as adolescents were all  delinquents we have poor judgment our  brains aren't fully developed and we  just need support you get through it and  let's explore that concept a little bit  so if we look at the cost and and we've  explored with adults that very often  when it comes to for example  homelessness it's actually cheaper to  provide housing and basic health care to  someone who's homeless then the social  costs over the course of a year from  that person being homeless we've talked  about adult education college education  is often less expensive than the cost of  incarceration  I'm guessing and I open it up to you to  tell me whether I'm right or wrong that  this is even Mystique wa li pronounced  when it comes to juveniles in terms of  education versus juvenile detention I  think it's much more pronounced  especially if you consider the  opportunity cost of a lost life we spend  eighty eight thousand dollars on average  to incarcerate a kid and ten thousand on  average to educate a single youth and  the kids know this the the message they  get from that is we'll spend anything to  contain you but nothing to cultivate you  and that's a very destructive message  when when the juvenile detention centers  are well I'll phrase it a different way  how prevalent is solitary confinement in  juvenile detention centers because  that's something that again on the adult  side we've talked about the destructive  effects long term of solitary  confinement and again from an outsider's  point of view I would imagine that  destructive effect would be even more  significant to a juvenile you're right I  mean the United Nations calls 15 hours  or more torture for adults and has no  any amount of time in solitary is  considered torture for children and  teenagers officially about a third of  juvenile detainees end up in some form  of solitary confinement  it's hard to measure because we don't  like to use that word we call it ad seg  or my favorite the reflection cottage in  among the kids I met and I talk to a lot  of kids I think I met maybe one or two  who hadn't experienced some form  solitary confinement or room isolation  one or two who had not experienced that  you're saying that's not empirical but  you know if you include things like  locking down a whole unit because  there's a shortage of teachers so you're  in your cell all day they had all  experienced that kind of intense  isolation and at least a third had  experienced formal solitary confinement  so a barren cell with maybe just a  blanket and now officially on the record  what are the reasons stated in some or  many of these cases how can we character  characterize the official explanations  for why this solitary confinement is  being assigned  well it's interesting Senator Durbin has  held a couple of hearings on this and  the official explanation given I think  by the head of the Federal Bureau of  Prisons and given by everyone under the  Sun was safety and security don't take  this away from us it's a tool that we  need but the kids to actually end up in  solitary for example then I heard this  over and over if you threaten worse if  you threaten suicide or act suicidal  you'll likely end up stripped naked and  tossed into a solitary cell where a  disproportionate number of suicides  unsurprisingly then occur if you're the  aggressor in a fight you could end up in  solitary if you're the victim of a fight  you could end up in solitary if they're  processing your papers you could end up  in solitary the number of reasons is  almost beyond reason really incredible I  want to remind everybody that the books  are burning down the house as well as  all alone in the world we've been  speaking with Nell Bernstein I recommend  both books now thank you so much for  being with us and really shedding light  onto something that many people may not  even be aware is going on  well thank you Oh is my pleasure

